    <h1>What Does a Baby Raven Look Like?</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Newly hatched ravens are naked, pinkish, and blind. Within a week they sprout dark pin feathers. By the time they leave the nest at 35–42 days, they are covered in dull brownish-black feathers, have a short tail, blue-grey eyes, and a pinkish gape — all features that distinguish them from the glossy, iridescent adults.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Hatchlings (Days 0–10)</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Raven eggs hatch after 20–21 days of incubation. The chicks emerge blind (eyes closed), nearly naked, and with pinkish-grey skin. They are entirely helpless — brooded continuously by the female while the male brings food. Within the first week, dark pin feathers begin to emerge through the skin, giving the chick a spiky appearance.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nestlings (Days 10–35)</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> As pin feathers open, the chick rapidly acquires a covering of dark brownish-black plumage — none of the iridescent gloss of adults. The eyes open between days 10 and 14. The bill gape — the fleshy pink or yellow flanges at the corners of the bill — is prominent and signals to parents where to deliver food. By three weeks the chick can thermoregulate and no longer needs constant brooding.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>The inside of the mouth is bright pink or red — a contrast that may help parents target food delivery in the dark nest interior. Nestlings are noisy, producing a distinctive "kra-kra" begging call that becomes louder and more insistent as they grow.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Fledglings (Weeks 5–10)</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Raven chicks fledge (leave the nest for the first time) at around 35–42 days old — roughly five to six weeks after hatching. At this stage they look like small, dull adults. Key distinguishing features:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Tail:</strong> shorter and squarer than the wedge-shaped tail of adults; the classic diamond or wedge shape develops over the first year.</li>
      <li><strong>Plumage:</strong> brownish-black, lacking the deep blue, violet and green iridescence of adult feathers.</li>
      <li><strong>Eyes:</strong> blue-grey or greyish, not the dark warm-brown of adults.</li>
      <li><strong>Gape:</strong> gape flanges may still be faintly visible at the bill corners.</li>
      <li><strong>Hackle:</strong> the elongated throat feathers (hackles) that form such a striking feature in adults are barely developed in first-year birds.</li>
      <li><strong>Bill:</strong> slightly smaller and less strongly curved than the adult's heavy bill.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Fledglings remain dependent on their parents for a further four to six weeks, following them and begging loudly while improving their flying and foraging skills.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Juvenile and First-Year Ravens</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> After independence, young ravens (sometimes called "juvenile" in their first year, then "immature" or "sub-adult" until full adult plumage) join non-breeding flocks — large, nomadic groups of young birds. First-year ravens are noticeably browner and duller than the glossy adults they encounter, and can be reliably aged in the field.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p>By their second and third year, iridescence begins to develop and the throat hackle becomes more pronounced. Full adult appearance is typically achieved by the second or third moult.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What to Do if You Find a Baby Raven</h2>
    <p>If you find a fledgling raven on the ground that is feathered and hopping, it is likely in the normal process of leaving the nest — parents are usually nearby. Leave it alone unless it is in immediate danger. If you find a nestling (naked or sparsely feathered, eyes closed), it has likely fallen from the nest and needs specialist help. Contact a licensed wildlife rehabilitator rather than attempting to care for it yourself — ravens are protected by law in most countries and require specialist care.</p>
  </section>

